Richard Pearson - Another Harrowing Episode of Cognitive Invasion
Pearson sounds the alarm - Mind control technology is out of control.
Dr. Richard Pearson, a PhD researcher who recently graduated from one of the most prestigious US research universities, has been tortured in the US for more than a year. His story is horrific. This essay focuses on a recent episode involving the use of mind invasion or control technology. Pearson explains later that the US and the world are in the Neuro Age and have been for at least 30 years, characterized by technology that invades the mind and body. The US government has not informed the public. Pearson is one of countless victims.
The Mysterious Van
Pearson sells art for a friend for money at a local market. It's not much but it helps with basic essentials. He might appear normal - unaffected - considering his torture account. "Far from it, I'm reliving the memories constantly -flashbacks of violence, stalking, harassment. It's really traumatic for me. Even people coming into the tent can be triggering. Street theater and mind control have persisted." Pearson sees more and more people losing a grip on reality. He's been physically attacked, electrocuted in his groin, threatened, and much more.
Pearson agrees to return his friend's van with the art to his friend's home in Maryland at the end of each shift. This has been his routine for years.
Last Sunday, September 20, Pearson returned the van. He noticed a work van in front of the neighbor's house next to his friend's house. "I have been to this man's house for years. That van had never been there, meaning the neighbor's house. In fact, I noticed "IT" on the vinyl car wrap advertising a business. I thought it was unusual because the older woman who lives there appears very much retired. What she'd need that for? Also, I had never seen any guests at the house, including that day." Pearson didn't think anything else of it outside of that. In fact, he thought he'd seen signs at the community prohibiting those kinds of work vehicles.
Pearson does some self-talk as he waits for a ride share. His verbal ruminations have been therapeutic in some ways. This day, he was practicing his French.
Thinking nothing of the van, Pearson readies for his ride share. It arrived. Pearson got into the car.
The Voice
Shortly after the driver pulled off, a voice in his head. Pearson was 2–4 houses from his friend's house. "J'ai oublié de vous dire." Pearson knows French. He can read and pronounce it well. Ever since Pearson began practicing his French, his tormenters switched languages too. The translation, "I forgot to tell you." A week prior, it was another French sentence as he was leaving H Street. "I have worked too damn hard all of my life. I'll be damn if someone if going to try to convince me that I am crazy when, in fact, I am being tortured in the nation's capital, no less."
"The voice was deeply unsettling as I rode in the ride share. Deeply. All sorts of things were running through my head. Were they listening to me in the van while I waited? Were my own thoughts and speech my own? Why was it so cryptic? Who are they? How are doing this? Why do they think it's ok?" Pearson attempted answers which he describes later.
Second Visit
The following week, Saturday, September 26, Pearson returned to his friend's house with the van. Same routine for years. The van was still there - on the street curb in front of the neighbor's house. The neighborhood had no sidewalks. Pearson is increasingly suspicious - the IT company, sudden appearance, and the voice the last visit. But he's too tired to ponder on it long. He tries for a quick nap in the van before calling a ride share. "I put down my head, laying across the driver's and passenger seats. But my mind isn't alone." Pearson explains it as someone on his radar. "It's wild! I know my own internal dialogue - my words, speech, and analysis. It's very difficult to explain, but it's like someone talking to me. One of the things that they say is that they are my rescuers. This is some really wild shit. I never get a nap. They don't shut up." Pearson explains that it's not like "voices" in a delusionary sense. He says that it's like his mind had been hijacked. There are no distinctive voices. It's his internal dialogue, but the thoughts are not self-generated.
Pearson will admit that he does want to be rescued. His account of torture is one of the most violent and sustained in the developed world post-WWII.
Pearson's friend was also selling that day but he was at another market that runs into the mid-eveing. The friend had a tent disaster revealed in the friend's texts - rain and wind from the nor'ester broke the runaway tent and drenched the art. Pearson offers to come there and help. The friend accepts. Pearson leaves in the van. Around Silver Spring, Pearson and his friend realize that Pearson's help isn't needed after all. The trip will take more than an hour. Pearson turns around - back to his friend's house.
He arrived then proceeded to wait in the van until the ride share arrived. It was a distinctly different experience. "I was alone in my head, as I should be!" Pearson looked over at the van. It had moved, but now it was parked at house 3 - two houses over.
"So get this? The van was parked on the street in front of my friend's immediate neighbor for a week or more, but now - an hour later - the van was in the driveway TWO houses down - a first. So it wasn't really belonging to the immediate neighbor."
Pearson is piecing a picture together. When he tried napping. This stream on his conscious. The van is parked on the street in clear view of Pearson who is parked at the end of the driveway that goes to the side of the house. Pearson returns a hour later. No stream. The van had moved to the driveway two houses down. It had no direct view of Pearson. Pearson generated an AI image to show it. The driveway where he was parked was actually on the other side of the house, much closer and in full view of the man.
The IT Company
Pearson gets out of the van to get the name of the company. His research reveals that the company claims many specializations, including cybersecurity. His interests piqued with another revelation. The man behind the company is from a French-speaking country. "It's awfully coincidental, right? The "J'ai oublié de vous dire" - French sentence and French-speaking figure behind company. When the van doesn't have direct sight of me in my friend's driveway - nothing on my radar when I returned." Pearson is not absolutely certain of his theory but there are enough anamolies to string together enough pearls. Pearson adds, "I have worked too damn hard all of my life. I got a PhD. I'll be damn if they are going to try to convince me that I'm crazy." Pearson also notes other instances of French inserted into his head, only after he began practicing his French as a distraction. He recently discussed an episode on H Street.
Pearson's Theories
How is this happening?
